    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a `bulkSignupWarning` flag to owner responses: **true** when more than 80 owners have already been registered for today, otherwise **false**.\n\nI kept structural impact low by putting the logic in small, cohesive units rather than growing the large controller class:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema with `default: false` and `readOnly: true`. Because the generated DTO field initializes to `false`, the `GET /api/owners/{id}` path (and any other owner read) reflects the correct default *with no code change* to those handlers \u2014 which is exactly what the acceptance test asserts.\n\n2. **`util/BulkSignup.java`** (new, tiny) \u2014 a single static `isWarning(Collection<Owner>)` that counts owners registered for today's business day and returns `count > 80`. It has essentially zero surrounding complexity (WMC_other \u2248 0), mirroring the existing `Namesakes`/`BusinessDay` utilities and reusing the same \"today\" accumulation path as the daily-limit rule.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 only two added lines: computing the flag from the pre-save owner collection (so \"more than 80 *already* created today\" excludes the new signup, matching the spec wording and the daily-limit rule's collection) and setting it on the DTO. Cyclomatic complexity of the method is unchanged (no new branches), and no new methods were added to the class, so `WMC_other` for surrounding methods stays flat.\n\nOnly 3 files touched; the sole edited method's complexity is unchanged and its edit is minimal, with the real work isolated in the new low-complexity helper.\n\nFull suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
